{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 33, :comments ["<b>And they brought him.</b> On the night after the Sabbath. <b>Those who found him.</b> It should have said אשר מצאו אותו — 'who found him'; rather, it teaches that they were still finding him gathering wood even after the warning, for they warned him and he would not stop himself from gathering." "<b>And you should be roused to ask</b> concerning what is mentioned in this passage several times the word אותו: ויקריבו אותו, המוצאים אותו, ויניחו אותו — 'they brought him,' 'those who found him,' 'they placed him' — and it did not say ויניחוהו as it said concerning the blasphemer; behold, they are included in the act of setting him apart. And further, why is he called by the expression מקושש — 'gatherer'? It should have said מלקט עצים — 'gathering wood' — on the Sabbath, for the expression of קשישה applies only to straw and stubble, as is said (Exodus 5:7): ״הם ילכו וקששו להם תבן״, and likewise (ibid.): ״לקושש קש לתבן״, whereas the expression of לקיטה applies to wood, as is said (Jeremiah 7:18): ״האבות מבערים את האש והבנים מלקטים עצים״." "<b>There is</b> a matter here. Know that the world has six directions, and it was created through the power of the name HaShem, the hidden one, with six seals, each direction with one seal, and it was created in six days. Now the Sabbath is called an אות, and this wood-gatherer denied that אות and the renewal of the world, and made the holy profane and the Sabbath like the other six. Therefore he is called מקושש — that is, מקו ששה — 'from the line of six,' for he departed from the middle line of the six directions and denied the seventh, which is the היכל הקדש, and understand this."], :book :numbers, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 15, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:32:48.997219744Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}